All four issues from this round are RESOLVED, verified headlessly (typecheck + build,
ar-verify Scene-Viewer gate, a 3D render QA of every avatar, and a state-siloing test),
and deployed.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. Print/printable-area math consistent across 2D ↔ 3D ↔ AR      ✅ RESOLVED
   Root cause: 2D and 3D already agreed (shared renderPrintArea + printAreaPx),
   but the AR *avatar* path was the outlier — it forced front AND back to one
   chestY (ignoring the per-side offset), shrank the print by a magic 0.82, and
   dropped the sleeve entirely.
   Fix: the avatar decal now uses the SAME per-side vertical offset as 2D/3D
   (areaOffsetYIn, now a single shared export in renderDesign.ts — no more
   copy-paste in Scene3D + arExport), draws the print at its TRUE inch size
   (only fit-clamped if it would overhang the torso), and projects the sleeve
   print onto the arms. Verified: back star sits at the nape, front at the
   chest, sleeve print present — matching the editor.

2. AR preview button in the 2D interface                          ✅ RESOLVED
   Added a "View in AR" CTA to the 2D editor (EditorCanvas), mirroring the 3D
   stage button and reusing the ar.view_in_ar i18n key; stacked above the
   mobile side-switcher so it doesn't collide.

3. Custom garment AR + "tee edits leak onto my custom garment"    ✅ RESOLVED
   (a) Leak: layers were a SINGLE shared list across every garment. Now the
       custom garment keeps its OWN design — `stashedLayers` holds the inactive
       context and swaps only when crossing the catalog↔custom boundary
       (tee↔hoodie still share, as before). Migrated so nothing is lost.
       Verified: editing the tee no longer changes the custom garment (and vice
       versa) — scripts/state-check.mjs.
   (b) Wrong garment in AR: custom loaded avatar-tee.glb and never read
       design.custom. Now buildCustomFigure renders the customer's ACTUAL
       uploaded garment (inflated shell of their photo + design, the same
       geometry the studio 3D uses), Scene-Viewer-safe. Verified: AR shows the
       uploaded garment shape (incl. neck cutout), not the default tee.

4. Realistic AR female model + male/female switch                 ✅ RESOLVED
   Generated neutral matte-gray featureless FEMALE mannequins (tee + hoodie,
   chroma-key green base) matching the male look; wired AVATAR_URL[gender][garment].
   Re-added the male/female toggle in the AR modal (shown for tee/hoodie; hidden
   for custom, which has no body). Each gender re-bakes → a new upload → a new
   QR/link automatically. Verified: female avatars load, recolour, and are
   Scene-Viewer-safe (ar-verify tee/female + hoodie/female).

Known-inherent (not blockers): faint mesh-edge speckles on the AI mannequin
bodies (single-image 3D); very dark studio colours (navy) read close to black
in dim light; a custom garment with no back photo shows a plain dark back.
